    "What a listener hears is not always what the page writes. Reading on, Hamzah pronounces every hamzah in full. When he stops on a word, a hamzah in its middle or at its end is eased, following the written form: turned into a long vowel, moved onto the letter before it, or softened. Every recording stops at the end of each ayah, so the last word of every ayah is heard without its hamzah (قَدۡ أَفۡلَحَ ٱلۡمُؤۡمِنُونَ ends in al-muuminoon), while a mushaf prints the continuous form and keeps it. The test is the same word mid-ayah, where the reciter keeps going: in 4:92 Khalaf reads li-mu'minin and mu'minan with the hamzah in full." : {},
